{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>Thus you shall bless the children of Israel.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): like the sense of זה תברכו, and similarly (Numbers 8:7) וכה תעשה להם לטהרם." "<b>And by way of the Midrash</b> — כה תברכו: the Holy One, blessed be He, delivered the blessings to the priests as a gift, that there should be power in their hands to bless Israel. And since He is destined to deliver to them twenty-four priestly gifts, and with this one they are twenty-five, therefore He employed this expression כה תברכו — meaning, together with the priestly blessing they are twenty-five. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: כה תברכו — with lifting of the hands; כה תברכו — in the holy tongue; כה תברכו — standing; כה תברכו — in a loud voice; כה תברכו — face to face; כה תברכו — in the Explicit Name. With lifting of the hands, as it is written (Leviticus 9:22) ״וישא אהרן את ידיו אל העם ויברכם״. In the holy tongue, standing, in a loud voice, as it is written (Deuteronomy 27:12) ״אלה יעמדו לברך את העם״ — just as there it is standing, in the holy tongue, and in a loud voice, so too here. Face to face, as it is written כה תברכו את בני ישראל אמור להם. In the Explicit Name: it is said here ״ושמו את שמי״ and it is said elsewhere (Deuteronomy 12:5) ״לשום את שמו שם״." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah</b> — כה תברכו: the word כה is the tenth sefirah, from within which the prophets always prophesy and say כה אמר ה׳; therefore He said כה תברכו, meaning: draw down the blessing from the attribute of kindness, which is the attribute of the priest, to the tenth. And with this the world would be blessed through the lifting of the priest's hands and the intention of his heart. And therefore you find the letter ה in the word כה תברכו, and it is an allusion to the final ה in the Name; and so David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 148:1) הללוהו במרומים, after he prefaced and said הללויה, in order to complete the unified ה-ה. And since the word כה is the attribute of softened judgment that guides Israel, and it is included within mercy, therefore when the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Abraham (Genesis 15:5) כה יהיה זרעך, He reckoned it to him as mercy, as it is stated (ibid.) ״ויחשבה לו צדקה״." "<b>Say to them.</b> אמור is spelled full (אמור מלא). The Holy One, blessed be He, said to the priests: not because I told you that you are to bless Israel should you bless them under compulsion and hastily, but rather you should bless them with intention of the heart; therefore it says אמור מלא.<br>And furthermore, אמור מלא can encompass that it is 248 together with the word, for the blessing is absorbed into the 248 limbs of a person; and the one missing is the limb of the heart, from which all the other limbs are strung and upon which all of them depend. And that which is hidden from the count is because it corresponds to the Shekhinah that rests upon the hands of the priest and is hidden there — for it is the heart of the heavens, from which the upper and lower beings are blessed.<br>And furthermore, אמור להם can encompass: in an unspecified manner, not explicitly — for there are times when the priests pronounce the Name with publicity during the lifting of the hands, and there are times when they do not publicize it but swallow it; and so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: when the transgressors multiplied, they would swallow it. And thus Scripture may say that there are times explicitly — this is כה תברכו את בני ישראל in the Explicit Name — and there are times in an unspecified manner, and this is אמור להם, in an unspecified manner.<br>And furthermore, אמור להם can encompass: if they are worthy — אמור להם; if they are not worthy — אמור in an unspecified manner, without להם. For Scripture ought to have said ותאמרו להם, but had it said thus, it would be implied that the blessing is drawn to them forever whether they are worthy or not worthy. Therefore it is written אמור להם, the meaning of which is: if they are worthy — אמור להם; if they are not worthy — אמור, in an unspecified manner, and not להם.<br>And I have already informed you in the pericope of ויבא עמלק that the priest, when he lifts [his hands toward] the ten, all ten are sanctified and the influx descends to the separate intellects, and the intellects and Israel are blessed — and this is כה תברכו את בני ישראל, the את being inclusive of the ministering angels; אמור להם — to all of them, above and below. And understand this.<br>And furthermore, one may interpret אמור מלא that the prayer leader (shaliah tzibbur) must recite the priestly blessing to the priests who lift their hands, and read it to them word by word, and they respond — and this is אמור, in the sense of a singular addressing the plural.<br>And that the shaliah tzibbur would read the three verses to the priest lifting his hands word by word — you can understand its virtue and its power, for such is the pattern of the priest above, who is blessed and receives the blessing from the essence of the supernal mercies and blesses Knesset Israel; and this is the secret of what our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: every priest who blesses is blessed, as it is said (Genesis 12:3) ״ואברכה מברכיך״ — and if so, everything is a pattern. And so the blessing cascades from the prayer leader, who wraps himself in a white tallit, to the priest, and from the priest to Israel — and this is clear.<br>And furthermore, אמור להם can encompass that the priests are obligated to bless Israel when Israel say to them [to do so] — and this is the expression אמור להם, and Onkelos translated כד יימרון להון. And after the invitation they transgress this positive commandment [if they do not bless], but without an invitation they do not transgress. For thus we find in the Jerusalem Talmud: Rabbi Shimon ben Pazi — when they were weary, one would stand behind the pillars; Rabbi Elazar would go outside. That is to say, they were weak and not one of them was able to lift his hands; one would hide among the pillars and the other would go outside the synagogue, so that they would not be invited to bless and be found transgressing this positive commandment if they did not bless after the invitation." "<b>And in the name of</b> R. Yitzhak ha-Zaken, Baal ha-Tosafot, of blessed memory, they said that a priest does not lift his hands unless he is married. And in truth it appears that there is a principle behind this, for a person is not complete without his mate, and he is unfit both to bless and to be blessed. And for this reason Nadab and Abihu deserved punishment, for they did not wish to marry, and the daughters of Israel were waiting for them and sat abandoned all their days. And I have already written that this is one of the six opinions that our Rabbis of blessed memory brought in their Midrash concerning their sin. And from this Scripture says (Psalms 78:63) ״בחוריו אכלה אש ובתולותיו לא הוללו״ — what is the reason בחוריו אכלה אש? Because בתולותיו לא הוללו. And from here they were punished according to one of the opinions, for they were not fit that Israel be blessed through them. Therefore the priest must be complete with his mate, after the pattern of the sefirot from which he draws down the blessing — and this is clear."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:30:21.371798171Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}